1
00:00:00,090 --> 00:00:03,440
Léa: Mathieu, I made a discovery this week.

2
00:00:03,440 --> 00:00:07,430
In the drawer of my desk.

3
00:00:07,480 --> 00:00:09,430
Mathieu: A twenty-euro note?

4
00:00:09,480 --> 00:00:13,070
Léa: Better. My New Year's resolutions notebook.

5
00:00:13,070 --> 00:00:16,030
The one from January 2026.

6
00:00:16,080 --> 00:00:18,070
Mathieu: Oh dear. And so?

7
00:00:18,120 --> 00:00:20,770
Léa: First page: nine resolutions,

8
00:00:20,770 --> 00:00:23,830
very nicely written, in colour.

9
00:00:23,880 --> 00:00:26,120
Mathieu: And the following pages?

10
00:00:26,170 --> 00:00:28,600
Léa: Empty. Except for one line.

11
00:00:28,600 --> 00:00:31,110
Written in March.

12
00:00:31,160 --> 00:00:33,910
Mathieu: Just one line? What does it say?

13
00:00:33,960 --> 00:00:36,950
Léa: I'll tell you at the end of the episode.

14
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:39,870
Mathieu: No! You can't do that to me.

15
00:00:39,920 --> 00:00:43,230
Léa: Yes. It's called suspense.

16
00:00:43,280 --> 00:00:46,430
Hello everyone and welcome to The Cozy French.

17
00:00:46,480 --> 00:00:47,910
I'm Léa.

18
00:00:47,960 --> 00:00:49,630
Mathieu: And I'm Mathieu.

19
00:00:49,680 --> 00:00:51,950
I'm going to spend twenty minutes wondering

20
00:00:51,950 --> 00:00:54,480
what's in that notebook.

21
00:00:54,530 --> 00:00:57,270
Léa: Perfect, that way you'll listen carefully.

22
00:00:57,320 --> 00:01:01,870
Today, we're talking about changing your life before 2027.

23
00:01:01,920 --> 00:01:02,280
Mathieu: Is that all?

24
00:01:02,280 --> 00:01:05,030
There are less than two months left.

25
00:01:05,080 --> 00:01:08,190
Léa: Exactly. Everyone waits for the first of January.

26
00:01:08,240 --> 00:01:10,470
We're asking ourselves a question:

27
00:01:10,520 --> 00:01:13,230
what if we started before?

28
00:01:13,280 --> 00:01:15,400
Mathieu: Don't worry, we're not going to ask you

29
00:01:15,400 --> 00:01:19,310
to get up at five to run ten kilometres.

30
00:01:19,360 --> 00:01:22,600
Léa: No. Rest isn't wasted time.

31
00:01:22,650 --> 00:01:23,880
We're talking more about the things

32
00:01:23,880 --> 00:01:27,470
that matter to you and that you always put off.

33
00:01:27,520 --> 00:01:31,670
Mathieu: This conversation is for levels B1 and B2.

34
00:01:31,720 --> 00:01:31,850
By the end,

35
00:01:31,850 --> 00:01:35,990
you'll be able to talk about your plans for next year,

36
00:01:36,040 --> 00:01:37,560
explain what holds you back

37
00:01:37,560 --> 00:01:40,710
and say what you're going to start.

38
00:01:40,760 --> 00:01:42,910
Léa: No need to understand every word.

39
00:01:42,960 --> 00:01:44,410
Follow the conversation,

40
00:01:44,410 --> 00:01:46,750
as if you were sitting with us.

41
00:01:46,800 --> 00:01:47,920
Mathieu: And if you recognise

42
00:01:47,920 --> 00:01:50,510
expressions from previous episodes,

43
00:01:50,510 --> 00:01:51,850
that's on purpose.

44
00:01:51,900 --> 00:01:55,910
It's by hearing them a second time that they stick.

45
00:01:55,960 --> 00:01:58,990
Léa: Before we start, tell us where you're listening from.

46
00:01:59,040 --> 00:02:01,270
We love imagining where you are.

47
00:02:01,320 --> 00:02:02,440
Mathieu: And if you too

48
00:02:02,440 --> 00:02:04,240
have a resolutions notebook

49
00:02:04,240 --> 00:02:06,110
sleeping in a drawer,

50
00:02:06,160 --> 00:02:07,480
give us a little thumbs-up.

51
00:02:07,480 --> 00:02:09,310
We don't judge.

52
00:02:09,360 --> 00:02:10,910
Léa: Especially not me.

53
00:02:10,960 --> 00:02:13,550
Mathieu: And keep this question in mind:

54
00:02:13,600 --> 00:02:17,350
what have you been putting off since the start of the year?

55
00:02:17,400 --> 00:02:19,200
Léa: Right. The notebook.

56
00:02:19,250 --> 00:02:22,510
On the first of January, I was full of energy.

57
00:02:22,560 --> 00:02:26,230
I had made good resolutions. Nine.

58
00:02:26,280 --> 00:02:29,150
Mathieu: Nine? Why not ten?

59
00:02:29,200 --> 00:02:32,710
Léa: The tenth was "be less ambitious".

60
00:02:32,760 --> 00:02:33,990
I crossed it out.

61
00:02:34,040 --> 00:02:36,550
Mathieu: Ha! Read me the list.

62
00:02:36,600 --> 00:02:40,270
Léa: Learn Spanish. Run three times a week.

63
00:02:40,320 --> 00:02:41,840
Read thirty books.

64
00:02:41,840 --> 00:02:43,470
Do pottery.

65
00:02:43,520 --> 00:02:44,640
Sort my photos.

66
00:02:44,640 --> 00:02:47,920
Call my grandmother every Sunday…

67
00:02:47,970 --> 00:02:50,230
Mathieu: That one's really sweet.

68
00:02:50,280 --> 00:02:52,730
Léa: Thanks. Eat less sugar.

69
00:02:52,730 --> 00:02:54,040
Learn to sew.

70
00:02:54,040 --> 00:02:57,870
And go to bed before eleven p.m.

71
00:02:57,920 --> 00:02:59,740
Mathieu: January Léa had big plans

72
00:02:59,740 --> 00:03:02,590
for February Léa.

73
00:03:02,640 --> 00:03:07,070
Léa: Huge. And February Léa hadn't asked for anything.

74
00:03:07,120 --> 00:03:09,550
Mathieu: And how long did it last?

75
00:03:09,600 --> 00:03:11,350
Léa: One week, very well.

76
00:03:11,400 --> 00:03:12,680
I ran twice,

77
00:03:12,680 --> 00:03:15,510
I downloaded a Spanish app,

78
00:03:15,560 --> 00:03:17,320
I bought some wool.

79
00:03:17,370 --> 00:03:20,870
Mathieu: Three beginnings in one week. Impressive.

80
00:03:20,920 --> 00:03:22,920
Léa: Yes, but the following Monday,

81
00:03:22,920 --> 00:03:25,310
I wanted to do everything on the same evening.

82
00:03:25,360 --> 00:03:28,590
Spanish, sewing, reading…

83
00:03:28,640 --> 00:03:31,190
At ten p.m., I was on the sofa,

84
00:03:31,240 --> 00:03:32,360
with a closed book

85
00:03:32,360 --> 00:03:35,190
and a ball of wool on my lap.

86
00:03:35,240 --> 00:03:36,910
Mathieu: You were spreading yourself too thin.

87
00:03:36,960 --> 00:03:39,710
Léa: Spreading myself too thin? Yes, that's the word.

88
00:03:39,760 --> 00:03:43,550
A little energy everywhere, and nothing really moving forward.

89
00:03:43,600 --> 00:03:45,240
Mathieu: And in February?

90
00:03:45,290 --> 00:03:45,920
Léa: In February,

91
00:03:45,920 --> 00:03:49,070
I stopped running because it was cold.

92
00:03:49,120 --> 00:03:52,870
In March, Spanish, because I'd missed three days.

93
00:03:52,920 --> 00:03:55,080
And the little character in the app

94
00:03:55,080 --> 00:03:58,110
cried every time I opened my phone.

95
00:03:58,160 --> 00:04:01,310
Mathieu: You felt guilty in front of a cartoon.

96
00:04:01,360 --> 00:04:02,990
Léa: Exactly.

97
00:04:03,040 --> 00:04:05,470
Mathieu: My grandfather always used to say:

98
00:04:05,520 --> 00:04:09,430
"You shouldn't chase two hares at once."

99
00:04:09,480 --> 00:04:12,440
Léa: Your grandfather hunted hares?

100
00:04:12,490 --> 00:04:16,390
Mathieu: No, he mostly hunted naps.

101
00:04:16,440 --> 00:04:19,360
But the idea is that by trying to catch everything,

102
00:04:19,360 --> 00:04:21,270
you catch nothing.

103
00:04:21,320 --> 00:04:23,710
Léa: Me, I was chasing nine.

104
00:04:23,760 --> 00:04:26,160
Mathieu: And what did you catch?

105
00:04:26,400 --> 00:04:29,870
Léa: A cold. From running in the rain.

106
00:04:29,920 --> 00:04:33,350
But wait, the best part is September.

107
00:04:33,400 --> 00:04:35,030
Mathieu: The return to school.

108
00:04:35,080 --> 00:04:36,670
Léa: The rentrée!

109
00:04:36,720 --> 00:04:40,600
You know, in France, we have two first of Januarys.

110
00:04:40,650 --> 00:04:44,510
Mathieu: The real one, and the second first of January:

111
00:04:44,560 --> 00:04:47,470
the September rentrée.

112
00:04:47,520 --> 00:04:52,310
Léa: Exactly. New diary, new pens, new life.

113
00:04:52,360 --> 00:04:54,790
So I bought a lovely diary…

114
00:04:54,790 --> 00:04:57,910
and I copied out the same list.

115
00:04:57,960 --> 00:04:59,470
Mathieu: All nine?

116
00:04:59,520 --> 00:05:03,070
Léa: All nine. In the same colours.

117
00:05:03,120 --> 00:05:05,710
I wanted to start again from scratch.

118
00:05:05,760 --> 00:05:10,590
Mathieu: Starting from scratch with exactly the same list.

119
00:05:10,640 --> 00:05:12,470
It's almost art.

120
00:05:12,520 --> 00:05:13,640
Léa: I laughed a lot finding

121
00:05:13,640 --> 00:05:16,150
the two notebooks side by side.

122
00:05:16,200 --> 00:05:19,430
But I also understood something.

123
00:05:19,480 --> 00:05:21,830
I didn't lack motivation.

124
00:05:21,880 --> 00:05:23,630
I lacked choices.

125
00:05:23,680 --> 00:05:26,310
Mathieu: Now that's a nice sentence.

126
00:05:26,360 --> 00:05:26,410
Léa: And you?

127
00:05:26,450 --> 00:05:27,630
You're not going to tell me

128
00:05:27,630 --> 00:05:31,030
you haven't put anything off this year.

129
00:05:31,080 --> 00:05:34,110
Mathieu: I have a very serious project.

130
00:05:34,160 --> 00:05:35,430
Bread.

131
00:05:35,480 --> 00:05:39,120
Léa: Bread? You want to make your own bread?

132
00:05:39,170 --> 00:05:41,310
Mathieu: Sourdough bread.

133
00:05:41,360 --> 00:05:43,070
Like at the local bakery,

134
00:05:43,070 --> 00:05:46,550
with the crust that sings when you cut it.

135
00:05:46,600 --> 00:05:48,830
Léa: The crust that sings…

136
00:05:48,880 --> 00:05:50,910
You've already convinced me.

137
00:05:50,960 --> 00:05:54,450
Mathieu: So, in spring, I watched a video.

138
00:05:54,450 --> 00:05:56,430
Then another.

139
00:05:56,480 --> 00:05:59,630
I think I watched about forty.

140
00:05:59,680 --> 00:06:02,310
Léa: Forty videos about bread?

141
00:06:02,360 --> 00:06:03,910
Mathieu: I know everything.

142
00:06:03,960 --> 00:06:09,190
Flour, water, temperature, resting time.

143
00:06:09,240 --> 00:06:11,750
I could give you a lecture.

144
00:06:11,800 --> 00:06:14,310
Léa: And how many loaves have you made?

145
00:06:14,360 --> 00:06:15,510
Mathieu: Zero.

146
00:06:15,560 --> 00:06:16,870
Léa: Zero?

147
00:06:16,920 --> 00:06:18,950
Mathieu: But I have a sourdough starter.

148
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:21,390
He's called Monsieur Bulle.

149
00:06:21,440 --> 00:06:22,520
He lives in my fridge,

150
00:06:22,520 --> 00:06:25,550
and I feed him every three days.

151
00:06:25,600 --> 00:06:29,470
Léa: Wait. You've been feeding a starter for months…

152
00:06:29,520 --> 00:06:31,710
without ever making bread?

153
00:06:31,760 --> 00:06:35,550
Mathieu: Let's say he's become a pet.

154
00:06:35,600 --> 00:06:38,760
Léa: Mathieu, that's the starter in the fridge.

155
00:06:38,810 --> 00:06:42,630
You look after the project, but you never use it.

156
00:06:42,680 --> 00:06:44,990
Mathieu: Ooh, that hurts.

157
00:06:45,040 --> 00:06:46,590
But it's true.

158
00:06:46,640 --> 00:06:49,710
Watching videos was pleasant.

159
00:06:49,760 --> 00:06:54,030
I felt I was making progress, without taking any risk.

160
00:06:54,080 --> 00:06:56,910
Léa: Because you never took the plunge.

161
00:06:56,960 --> 00:06:59,470
Mathieu: Taking the plunge… Yes.

162
00:06:59,520 --> 00:07:03,070
Stop preparing, and really do the thing.

163
00:07:03,120 --> 00:07:05,280
Léa: And what changed?

164
00:07:05,330 --> 00:07:06,690
Mathieu: Two weeks ago,

165
00:07:06,690 --> 00:07:08,880
my upstairs neighbour brought me

166
00:07:08,880 --> 00:07:11,830
a loaf she'd made herself.

167
00:07:11,880 --> 00:07:14,510
She'd started in September.

168
00:07:14,560 --> 00:07:16,790
With just one video.

169
00:07:16,840 --> 00:07:18,230
Léa: Just one?

170
00:07:18,280 --> 00:07:22,510
Mathieu: Just one. And her bread was magnificent.

171
00:07:22,560 --> 00:07:24,870
That's when it clicked for me.

172
00:07:24,920 --> 00:07:27,030
Léa: The famous click.

173
00:07:27,080 --> 00:07:30,750
Mathieu: The following Saturday, I made my first loaf.

174
00:07:30,800 --> 00:07:32,350
Léa: And so?

175
00:07:32,400 --> 00:07:35,550
Mathieu: It was flat. Really flat.

176
00:07:35,600 --> 00:07:39,270
It looked like a slightly sad crêpe.

177
00:07:39,320 --> 00:07:40,310
Léa: Oh no…

178
00:07:40,360 --> 00:07:42,570
Mathieu: But with a piece of Comté,

179
00:07:42,570 --> 00:07:45,790
I swear, it was delicious.

180
00:07:45,840 --> 00:07:46,370
And above all,

181
00:07:46,370 --> 00:07:50,830
I learned more in one hour than in forty videos.

182
00:07:50,880 --> 00:07:51,910
Léa: Because now,

183
00:07:51,910 --> 00:07:54,710
you know what's difficult for you.

184
00:07:54,760 --> 00:07:57,710
Mathieu: The dough. It sticks everywhere.

185
00:07:57,760 --> 00:08:02,150
To my hands, the worktop, my phone…

186
00:08:02,200 --> 00:08:04,200
Léa: To your phone?

187
00:08:04,250 --> 00:08:06,990
Mathieu: I wanted to watch a video again.

188
00:08:07,040 --> 00:08:08,270
Léa: Of course.

189
00:08:08,320 --> 00:08:10,790
And it's the same with French, isn't it?

190
00:08:10,840 --> 00:08:14,070
You can know all the rules of the passé composé…

191
00:08:14,120 --> 00:08:17,470
Mathieu: …and still get stuck when someone asks:

192
00:08:17,520 --> 00:08:19,510
"What did you do this weekend?"

193
00:08:19,560 --> 00:08:22,270
Léa: Exactly. The rule is the video.

194
00:08:22,320 --> 00:08:25,390
The conversation is the bread.

195
00:08:25,440 --> 00:08:27,390
Mathieu: Even if it's a bit flat.

196
00:08:27,440 --> 00:08:29,680
But why do we do this?

197
00:08:29,730 --> 00:08:32,930
Why do we always wait for the first of January,

198
00:08:32,930 --> 00:08:34,470
or a Monday?

199
00:08:34,520 --> 00:08:35,800
Léa: I think a date

200
00:08:35,800 --> 00:08:38,590
gives us the feeling of a blank page.

201
00:08:38,640 --> 00:08:41,110
On the first of January, everything is clean.

202
00:08:41,160 --> 00:08:43,470
We haven't failed at anything yet.

203
00:08:43,520 --> 00:08:45,600
Mathieu: Whereas on a Wednesday in November,

204
00:08:45,600 --> 00:08:49,830
at six p.m., it's raining, we're tired…

205
00:08:49,880 --> 00:08:50,930
Léa: …and we've already missed

206
00:08:50,930 --> 00:08:53,150
lots of things during the week.

207
00:08:53,200 --> 00:08:56,070
It doesn't look like the start of a new life.

208
00:08:56,120 --> 00:08:58,040
Mathieu: And there's something else.

209
00:08:58,090 --> 00:09:01,110
When you start, you can fail.

210
00:09:01,160 --> 00:09:05,150
As long as you wait for January, the project stays perfect.

211
00:09:05,200 --> 00:09:08,870
Léa: In our heads, later on, we're always ready.

212
00:09:08,920 --> 00:09:12,190
Mathieu: Later on, I make a magnificent loaf.

213
00:09:12,240 --> 00:09:15,050
Léa: Later on, I speak Spanish with my grandmother…

214
00:09:15,050 --> 00:09:16,640
who doesn't speak Spanish.

215
00:09:16,640 --> 00:09:20,110
Mathieu: Ha! And then, we imagine things.

216
00:09:20,160 --> 00:09:21,270
Léa: Imagine things?

217
00:09:21,320 --> 00:09:24,400
Mathieu: We imagine things that don't exist.

218
00:09:24,450 --> 00:09:25,810
"If my bread fails,

219
00:09:25,810 --> 00:09:28,870
my neighbour will think I'm useless."

220
00:09:28,920 --> 00:09:30,600
Léa: Even though she brought you bread.

221
00:09:30,600 --> 00:09:33,390
She'd rather you succeed.

222
00:09:33,440 --> 00:09:37,870
Mathieu: Yes, put like that, my film isn't very realistic.

223
00:09:37,920 --> 00:09:41,270
Léa: For me, it mostly happens after a conversation.

224
00:09:41,320 --> 00:09:43,230
Someone makes a little remark,

225
00:09:43,230 --> 00:09:46,270
and I go over it again and again for three days.

226
00:09:46,320 --> 00:09:48,710
Mathieu: Ressasser? Go on.

227
00:09:48,760 --> 00:09:53,720
Léa: Thinking about the same thing, over and over, without moving forward.

228
00:09:53,770 --> 00:09:55,680
In the evening, while doing the dishes,

229
00:09:55,680 --> 00:09:58,560
I prepare the perfect answer.

230
00:09:58,560 --> 00:10:00,430
Three days too late.

231
00:10:00,480 --> 00:10:01,600
Mathieu: And all that energy,

232
00:10:01,600 --> 00:10:04,070
doesn't go into the project.

233
00:10:04,120 --> 00:10:07,740
Léa: Exactly. We spend a lot of energy thinking,

234
00:10:07,740 --> 00:10:10,510
and very little is left for doing.

235
00:10:10,560 --> 00:10:11,190
Right, careful.

236
00:10:11,190 --> 00:10:14,670
We're not saying January is a bad idea.

237
00:10:14,720 --> 00:10:17,110
If January helps you, great.

238
00:10:17,160 --> 00:10:20,320
Mathieu: And sometimes, waiting is reasonable.

239
00:10:20,370 --> 00:10:24,110
A move, a baby, exams…

240
00:10:24,160 --> 00:10:26,230
Léa: The real question is rather:

241
00:10:26,280 --> 00:10:27,320
is there

242
00:10:27,320 --> 00:10:31,110
a tiny version I can start now?

243
00:10:31,160 --> 00:10:33,630
Just to get a foot in the door.

244
00:10:33,680 --> 00:10:34,780
Mathieu: Like on a horse.

245
00:10:34,780 --> 00:10:38,590
Once your foot is in, the rest gets easier.

246
00:10:38,640 --> 00:10:40,190
Léa: Shall we try something?

247
00:10:40,240 --> 00:10:43,750
In a few weeks, it's Christmas dinner with the family.

248
00:10:43,800 --> 00:10:45,100
Mathieu: The moment when everyone

249
00:10:45,100 --> 00:10:48,400
asks questions about your plans.

250
00:10:48,450 --> 00:10:51,160
Léa: Exactly. I'm your aunt Monique.

251
00:10:51,160 --> 00:10:53,150
We're at the cheese course.

252
00:10:53,200 --> 00:10:55,350
And I say to you, in front of everyone:

253
00:10:55,350 --> 00:10:58,870
"So, Mathieu, that famous bread, how's it going?"

254
00:10:58,920 --> 00:11:01,110
Mathieu: I won't answer right away.

255
00:11:01,160 --> 00:11:03,610
You, in my place, what would you say?

256
00:11:03,660 --> 00:11:05,950
Take a few seconds.

257
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:09,270
Léa: There. Now, Mathieu's version.

258
00:11:09,320 --> 00:11:12,270
So, that famous bread, how's it going?

259
00:11:12,320 --> 00:11:14,230
Mathieu: It's coming along slowly!

260
00:11:14,280 --> 00:11:18,220
The first one was a bit flat, but delicious.

261
00:11:18,270 --> 00:11:20,390
On Saturday, I'm making another one.

262
00:11:20,440 --> 00:11:23,070
If you like, I'll save you a piece.

263
00:11:23,120 --> 00:11:25,950
Léa: Oh, that's a very good answer.

264
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:26,950
Mathieu: Why?

265
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:29,800
Léa: You're honest, you make a little joke about yourself,

266
00:11:29,800 --> 00:11:33,230
and you give a next step: Saturday.

267
00:11:33,280 --> 00:11:36,280
Mathieu: And on top of that, I give aunt Monique a present.

268
00:11:36,280 --> 00:11:38,630
She can't say anything anymore.

269
00:11:38,680 --> 00:11:41,330
Léa: But you know, some people answer:

270
00:11:41,330 --> 00:11:43,160
"Bread? At your age?

271
00:11:43,160 --> 00:11:45,600
Just go to the bakery."

272
00:11:45,690 --> 00:11:48,710
Mathieu: Ah, yes. After that kind of conversation,

273
00:11:48,710 --> 00:11:51,950
you feel less like doing anything at all.

274
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:53,560
Léa: And others just ask you:

275
00:11:53,560 --> 00:11:56,270
"What's the next step?"

276
00:11:56,320 --> 00:11:59,950
And suddenly, it seems possible.

277
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:04,430
Mathieu: Enthusiasm is contagious. So is discouragement.

278
00:12:04,480 --> 00:12:07,030
Léa: So here's a good question to ask yourself:

279
00:12:07,080 --> 00:12:08,920
after talking with this person,

280
00:12:08,920 --> 00:12:13,040
do I feel more like it, or less?

281
00:12:13,090 --> 00:12:16,070
Mathieu: With you, I feel like making bread.

282
00:12:16,120 --> 00:12:18,160
Léa: And I feel like eating some.

283
00:12:18,160 --> 00:12:20,430
Everyone wins.

284
00:12:20,480 --> 00:12:21,640
Mathieu: In French, it's the same.

285
00:12:21,640 --> 00:12:23,130
Surround yourself with people

286
00:12:23,130 --> 00:12:26,190
who let you finish your sentence.

287
00:12:26,240 --> 00:12:28,590
Léa: Even when it's a bit flat.

288
00:12:28,640 --> 00:12:31,980
Mathieu: Right. In practical terms, there are less than two months left.

289
00:12:31,980 --> 00:12:33,510
What do we do?

290
00:12:33,560 --> 00:12:36,270
Léa: First idea: one single thing.

291
00:12:36,270 --> 00:12:38,590
Not nine. One.

292
00:12:38,640 --> 00:12:41,320
Mathieu: Just one? That seems very little to me.

293
00:12:41,370 --> 00:12:42,690
Léa: It's not for your whole life.

294
00:12:42,690 --> 00:12:44,590
It's until the first of January.

295
00:12:44,640 --> 00:12:48,350
The other ideas stay in the notebook, nice and cosy.

296
00:12:48,400 --> 00:12:50,710
Mathieu: Like Monsieur Bulle in the fridge.

297
00:12:50,760 --> 00:12:51,100
Léa: Exactly. Me,

298
00:12:51,100 --> 00:12:55,070
I chose to call my grandmother every Sunday.

299
00:12:55,120 --> 00:12:58,030
I'm sticking to that until the end of the year.

300
00:12:58,080 --> 00:12:59,990
Mathieu: Sticking to one single thing…

301
00:12:59,990 --> 00:13:02,310
It's almost restful.

302
00:13:02,360 --> 00:13:05,990
Léa: Very. I no longer need to choose every evening.

303
00:13:06,040 --> 00:13:07,270
Mathieu: Second idea?

304
00:13:07,320 --> 00:13:09,880
Léa: Stop saying "I don't have time".

305
00:13:09,930 --> 00:13:11,950
Free time doesn't fall from the sky.

306
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:15,190
You book it, like a doctor's appointment.

307
00:13:15,240 --> 00:13:16,830
Mathieu: A time slot, in fact.

308
00:13:16,880 --> 00:13:20,350
Léa: A slot, yes: a precise moment in the diary.

309
00:13:20,400 --> 00:13:21,010
On Sunday,

310
00:13:21,010 --> 00:13:25,230
from six to six-thirty p.m., it's Grandma.

311
00:13:25,280 --> 00:13:27,110
Mathieu: So I tried it.

312
00:13:27,160 --> 00:13:30,440
I decided to devote my Sunday morning to bread.

313
00:13:30,440 --> 00:13:31,550
At seven o'clock.

314
00:13:31,550 --> 00:13:34,270
Léa: Seven o'clock? You?

315
00:13:34,320 --> 00:13:38,440
Mathieu: Saturday-night Mathieu thought it was an excellent idea.

316
00:13:38,490 --> 00:13:40,670
Léa: And Sunday-morning Mathieu?

317
00:13:40,720 --> 00:13:42,170
Mathieu: He switched off the alarm

318
00:13:42,170 --> 00:13:45,030
and sent an out-of-office message.

319
00:13:45,080 --> 00:13:46,910
Léa: Then we change the slot.

320
00:13:46,960 --> 00:13:48,870
Saturday afternoon, one loaf.

321
00:13:48,870 --> 00:13:50,990
Not three. One.

322
00:13:51,040 --> 00:13:53,750
Mathieu: Saturday afternoon, that I can do.

323
00:13:53,800 --> 00:13:57,190
Léa: Third idea, and maybe the most important:

324
00:13:57,240 --> 00:14:00,190
hold on when it gets boring.

325
00:14:00,240 --> 00:14:04,030
Mathieu: Boring? But at the start, it's exciting.

326
00:14:04,080 --> 00:14:05,200
Léa: At the start, yes.

327
00:14:05,200 --> 00:14:07,800
New notebook, new flour.

328
00:14:07,850 --> 00:14:09,840
But after two or three weeks,

329
00:14:09,840 --> 00:14:11,670
nobody applauds you anymore.

330
00:14:11,720 --> 00:14:14,390
That's when a lot of people give up.

331
00:14:14,440 --> 00:14:16,510
Mathieu: A bit like bread rising.

332
00:14:16,560 --> 00:14:17,170
For hours,

333
00:14:17,170 --> 00:14:19,950
you feel like nothing is happening.

334
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:23,590
Léa: And yet, lots of things are happening inside.

335
00:14:23,640 --> 00:14:24,960
You just mustn't open

336
00:14:24,960 --> 00:14:27,950
the oven every five minutes.

337
00:14:28,000 --> 00:14:29,060
Mathieu: For French,

338
00:14:29,060 --> 00:14:31,510
it could be twenty minutes out loud,

339
00:14:31,560 --> 00:14:34,760
three times a week, until the end of the year.

340
00:14:34,810 --> 00:14:37,380
Léa: Rather than two hours on the first of January…

341
00:14:37,380 --> 00:14:39,350
and nothing by the fourth.

342
00:14:39,400 --> 00:14:41,190
Mathieu: And what if you miss a day?

343
00:14:41,240 --> 00:14:42,690
Léa: You don't start from scratch.

344
00:14:42,690 --> 00:14:44,390
You pick up again the next day.

345
00:14:44,440 --> 00:14:47,230
It's not an exam, it's a habit.

346
00:14:47,280 --> 00:14:48,310
Over to you now.

347
00:14:48,360 --> 00:14:50,550
Here's a sentence to complete:

348
00:14:50,600 --> 00:14:53,390
"By 2027, I want…

349
00:14:53,440 --> 00:14:55,390
To do that, I'm going to…"

350
00:14:55,440 --> 00:15:00,350
Mathieu: Me: by 2027, I want to make a real loaf.

351
00:15:00,400 --> 00:15:04,200
To do that, I'll make one every Saturday afternoon.

352
00:15:04,250 --> 00:15:06,520
Léa: And me: by 2027,

353
00:15:06,520 --> 00:15:09,190
I want to talk more often with my grandmother.

354
00:15:09,240 --> 00:15:09,600
To do that,

355
00:15:09,600 --> 00:15:13,550
I'll call her every Sunday at six p.m.

356
00:15:13,600 --> 00:15:14,950
Mathieu: Did you notice?

357
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:18,030
One single thing, and a precise time slot.

358
00:15:18,080 --> 00:15:19,680
Léa: If you're learning French,

359
00:15:19,680 --> 00:15:22,310
your sentence can be about French.

360
00:15:22,360 --> 00:15:26,710
"By 2027, I want to understand a film without subtitles.

361
00:15:26,760 --> 00:15:30,470
To do that, I'll watch ten minutes a day."

362
00:15:30,520 --> 00:15:32,440
Mathieu: Ten minutes is small.

363
00:15:32,490 --> 00:15:34,400
But ten minutes every day

364
00:15:34,400 --> 00:15:36,430
adds up to a lot of films.

365
00:15:36,480 --> 00:15:38,830
Léa: Your turn. Say your sentence out loud.

366
00:15:38,880 --> 00:15:40,750
Take a few seconds.

367
00:15:40,800 --> 00:15:42,750
Mathieu: No need for a perfect sentence.

368
00:15:42,800 --> 00:15:46,270
A simple sentence is already a sentence in French.

369
00:15:46,320 --> 00:15:48,670
Léa: Keep it safe. At the end of the episode,

370
00:15:48,670 --> 00:15:51,110
we'll add something to it.

371
00:15:51,160 --> 00:15:53,430
Mathieu: Let's go back over a few expressions.

372
00:15:53,480 --> 00:15:56,030
Listen, then repeat with us.

373
00:15:56,080 --> 00:15:56,600
Léa: Earlier,

374
00:15:56,600 --> 00:15:58,480
I was telling you about my nine resolutions

375
00:15:58,480 --> 00:16:00,420
and Mathieu's grandfather.

376
00:16:00,470 --> 00:16:04,230
The expression was: courir deux lièvres à la fois.

377
00:16:04,280 --> 00:16:07,190
Mathieu: It means wanting to do several things at once,

378
00:16:07,240 --> 00:16:10,150
and in the end, succeeding at none.

379
00:16:10,200 --> 00:16:13,040
Example: he's preparing for a competitive exam

380
00:16:13,040 --> 00:16:15,510
and moving house the same month.

381
00:16:15,560 --> 00:16:18,270
He's chasing two hares at once.

382
00:16:18,320 --> 00:16:23,160
Léa: Let's repeat: courir deux lièvres à la fois.

383
00:16:23,640 --> 00:16:27,740
Mathieu: Next, my forty videos and Monsieur Bulle.

384
00:16:27,790 --> 00:16:30,950
The expression was: passer à l'acte.

385
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:33,800
Léa: It means stopping preparing or talking about it,

386
00:16:33,800 --> 00:16:36,190
and really doing the thing.

387
00:16:36,240 --> 00:16:40,110
Example: she'd been talking about this trip for years,

388
00:16:40,160 --> 00:16:42,910
and this summer, she finally did it.

389
00:16:42,960 --> 00:16:46,230
Mathieu: Let's repeat: passer à l'acte.

390
00:16:46,280 --> 00:16:49,430
Léa: When Mathieu was afraid his neighbour would think he was useless,

391
00:16:49,480 --> 00:16:52,470
we said: se faire des films.

392
00:16:52,520 --> 00:16:55,760
Mathieu: It means imagining things, often negative,

393
00:16:55,760 --> 00:16:58,070
that aren't real.

394
00:16:58,120 --> 00:17:01,150
Example: he hasn't replied to me yet,

395
00:17:01,200 --> 00:17:04,110
but I'm not going to imagine things.

396
00:17:04,160 --> 00:17:06,230
He's surely busy.

397
00:17:06,280 --> 00:17:10,190
Léa: Let's repeat: se faire des films.

398
00:17:10,240 --> 00:17:12,220
Mathieu: For the bread that rises slowly,

399
00:17:12,220 --> 00:17:15,310
we talked about tenir bon.

400
00:17:15,360 --> 00:17:18,730
Léa: It means carrying on despite difficulty or tiredness,

401
00:17:18,730 --> 00:17:20,750
without giving up.

402
00:17:20,800 --> 00:17:24,590
Example: the last week on the building site was hard,

403
00:17:24,640 --> 00:17:27,080
but the whole team held on.

404
00:17:27,130 --> 00:17:29,590
Mathieu: Let's repeat: tenir bon.

405
00:17:29,640 --> 00:17:32,040
Léa: And finally, for the people who make us want to, or not:

406
00:17:32,040 --> 00:17:34,470
contagious.

407
00:17:34,520 --> 00:17:39,310
Mathieu: An illness can be contagious, but so can a laugh.

408
00:17:39,360 --> 00:17:42,910
Example: her good mood is contagious,

409
00:17:42,960 --> 00:17:45,990
the whole office smiles on Monday morning.

410
00:17:46,040 --> 00:17:50,070
Léa: Let's repeat: contagieux, contagieuse.

411
00:17:50,120 --> 00:17:52,230
Mathieu: And now, say it differently.

412
00:17:52,280 --> 00:17:56,640
I say a simple sentence, Léa says the natural version.

413
00:17:56,690 --> 00:17:57,450
Léa: Go ahead.

414
00:17:57,500 --> 00:18:01,190
Mathieu: "I start everything and finish nothing."

415
00:18:01,240 --> 00:18:02,690
Léa: I'm spreading myself too thin.

416
00:18:02,720 --> 00:18:05,030
Mathieu: "I don't have time for that."

417
00:18:05,080 --> 00:18:07,090
Léa: I can't manage to find a slot.

418
00:18:07,140 --> 00:18:11,110
Mathieu: "I suddenly understood I had to do it."

419
00:18:11,160 --> 00:18:12,610
Léa: It clicked for me.

420
00:18:12,650 --> 00:18:16,110
Mathieu: "I think about that argument all the time."

421
00:18:16,160 --> 00:18:18,030
Léa: I keep going over it.

422
00:18:18,080 --> 00:18:20,860
Mathieu: And a very useful sentence in conversation:

423
00:18:20,860 --> 00:18:23,110
"I didn't understand."

424
00:18:23,160 --> 00:18:24,790
Léa: Sorry, I didn't quite catch that.

425
00:18:24,790 --> 00:18:26,790
Could you repeat?

426
00:18:26,840 --> 00:18:29,610
Mathieu: With this sentence, you stay in the conversation,

427
00:18:29,610 --> 00:18:32,070
even when it goes too fast.

428
00:18:32,120 --> 00:18:34,030
Léa: A little tip for this episode:

429
00:18:34,030 --> 00:18:37,190
listen to it once without stopping,

430
00:18:37,240 --> 00:18:40,310
then a second time with the subtitles.

431
00:18:40,360 --> 00:18:42,120
Mathieu: Choose a short exchange,

432
00:18:42,120 --> 00:18:44,670
for example with aunt Monique,

433
00:18:44,670 --> 00:18:47,190
and repeat it with us.

434
00:18:47,240 --> 00:18:50,070
Léa: And now, take your sentence from earlier,

435
00:18:50,070 --> 00:18:55,050
and add a reason: "By 2027, I want…

436
00:18:55,050 --> 00:18:58,590
To do that, I'm going to… because…"

437
00:18:58,640 --> 00:19:03,720
Mathieu: Me: by 2027, I want to make a real loaf,

438
00:19:03,720 --> 00:19:08,430
because I finally want to hear the crust sing.

439
00:19:08,480 --> 00:19:10,870
Léa: Write your sentence in the comments.

440
00:19:10,870 --> 00:19:12,470
We read them all.

441
00:19:12,520 --> 00:19:14,650
Mathieu: And send this episode to someone

442
00:19:14,650 --> 00:19:16,880
who has a starter in the fridge.

443
00:19:16,880 --> 00:19:18,640
They'll understand.

444
00:19:18,690 --> 00:19:20,590
Léa: If this conversation helped you,

445
00:19:20,590 --> 00:19:22,870
subscribe to The Cozy French.

446
00:19:22,920 --> 00:19:25,430
Take care of yourselves and see you very soon!

447
00:19:25,480 --> 00:19:26,550
Mathieu: Goodbye everyone!

448
00:19:26,600 --> 00:19:28,870
Wait… Léa. The notebook.

449
00:19:28,920 --> 00:19:32,120
Léa: Oh yes. The famous line from March.

450
00:19:32,170 --> 00:19:32,950
Mathieu: So?

451
00:19:33,000 --> 00:19:34,840
Léa: "Buy a new notebook."

452
00:19:34,890 --> 00:19:35,830
Mathieu: No!

453
00:19:35,880 --> 00:19:37,320
Léa: Yes. And I bought it.

454
00:19:37,320 --> 00:19:38,840
In September.

455
00:19:38,890 --> 00:19:42,240
Mathieu: So that's the only resolution you kept.

456
00:19:42,290 --> 00:19:45,440
Léa: Exactly. One hundred percent success.

457
00:19:45,490 --> 00:19:48,830
Mathieu: Right. I'm going to feed Monsieur Bulle.

458
00:19:48,830 --> 00:19:51,480
On Saturday, he's working.
